{
  "requirement": "When given `--path <crate-dir>` and no existing Habeas dev dependency, `habeas setup` MUST add that local crate as the dev dependency. Repeating setup MUST NOT add a duplicate Habeas dependency.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Implementation: sound and marked.** I found no unmarked implementation and no unspecified behavior.\n- `src/main.rs` defines `setup --path <crate-dir>` with clap and passes it to `setup::run`.\n- `src/setup.rs` `has_habeas_dev_dependency` (marked {impl}) checks `[dev-dependencies].habeas` in the manifest.\n- `run` (with the {impl} marker at line ~161) skips `cargo add` when that dependency exists. Otherwise it runs `cargo add --dev habeas --manifest-path … --path <canonicalized crate-dir>`. That adds the local crate as the dev dependency, and a repeated run does not add it again.\n\n**Tests: too weak to check what the requirement says.** The only {test} reference is `tests/setup.rs::setup_is_repeatable`. It runs `setup --path $CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` twice and then asserts only these things about the dependency:\n- `Cargo.toml` contains `[dev-dependencies]`.\n- `Cargo.toml` contains the substring `habeas`.\n- `Cargo.toml` contains the substring `path = `.\n\nIt does not check these points:\n- The dependency's `path` is the crate directory passed to `--path`. This is the core obligation, \"add that local crate\".\n- Exactly one `habeas` dependency entry exists after the second run. The \"no duplicate\" obligation is only covered because the second run does not fail.\n- An existing Habeas dev dependency is left untouched, for example one that points at a different path. That is the branch where `cargo add` is skipped, and the test never sets it up. Only the Python Symposium smoke test starts from a manifest that already has a path dependency, and it does not check that the manifest is unchanged.\n\nThese assertions would still pass if `--path` were ignored and some other `path = ` entry were added. They also would not catch an added second entry. So the tests do not cover the interesting points of the requirement, and I am judging it false on that basis.\n\nNo FIXME acknowledges these gaps.",
